hungry  there's always something to eat  here in the settlement you die with a  full stomach  in brazil many dream of owning their own  property life in the country offers a  radical change from brazil's overcrowded  mega cities where there's little room  and growth is almost uncontrolled  for some getting back to nature offers  the best chance to improve their living  conditions  it's a new chapter of my life  i'm building something here that i'll  have for as long as i live  it's my future my baby  when i die i'll leave something behind  for my son  this land is everything for me  that was your dream right  exactly  now you have to learn how to cultivate  it yes  today in the 21st century it's almost  impossible to create a settlement for  people without land without giving the  farmers the proper resources  they need to work with the latest  technology  to keep up with the pace of change  and bring their methods in line with  proper environmental practices  we believe that sustainable or as we  call it in latin america agroecological  farming is an important tool  we want an agricultural industry that's  independent from chemical fertilizers  pesticides and herbicides  in other words an agricultural system  that produces food and protects the  environment the  also wants to help settlers build a  better community  our real goal is to help people create  the right foundation to develop their  land  and of course it should have an  agroecological focus  the production should help improve  conditions for the entire community  and not focus on competing on the market  our production principles focus on the  proper treatment of the planet  exchanging information and ideas is  critical for settlers hoping to survive  in the country just like learning to  plant crops on each of these small farms  students professors and farmers are  working together to make sure that the  land can once again support sustainable  agriculture  and finally we take a look at an  important anniversary and an effort to  honor war heroes it was 100 years ago  that the first balkan war broke out  between the ottoman empire and an  alliance of four countries one of which  was bulgaria  hundreds of thousands of bulgarian men  were called up to fight yet over the  last century many of their names and  their sacrifices have been forgotten  that's where the bulgarian state archive  comes in the agency is trying to restore  all of the fallen soldiers records and  make sure that bulgarians today honor  their service  the year is 1912 the place the bulgarian  capital of sofia onlookers are reading a  poster describing the mobilization for  the first balkan war the call is out for  fresh recruits in just a week's time the  bulgarian army would swell to 600 000  soldiers with more than 1 100 heavy  weapons  on september 29th the bulgarian army set  up camp in the town of stara zagora  under the command of king ferdinand  bulgaria started military operations on  october the fifth  eleven days later the first bulgarian  reconnaissance flight took place  pilot radu milkov accompanied by prado  anthrachev took off from sveilingrad  airport in an albatross f2 biplane  during their 50 minutes in the air over  the region of ordering they were able to  locate ottoman forces and attack an  important target a military camp on the  karaka river the conflict between the  ottoman empire and an alliance of  bulgarian serbian greek and montenegrin  forces ended one year later with a  victory for the allies  it's been 100 years since the first  balkan war ended  i still don't know the names of the real  heroes of that conflict  there is a monument in the center of  sofia dedicated to the unknown soldiers  but it only demonstrates our lack of  knowledge  in the 1930s the ministry of war started  to prepare a complete and detailed list  of all the dead bulgarian heroes of the  balkan wars  the list fills more than 30 volumes and  took over a year to digitize  here in front of us we can see one of  the books of names which we digitalized  in it we can find a wealth of  information  not only about the fallen soldiers but  also where they perished and their final  resting places  this will allow there some 250 000  descendants to find the graves of their  great grandfathers with just a few mouse  clicks  if they want they can pay their respects  by lighting a candle or laying a wreath  in this way we hope to restore their  interrupted family histories  the bulgarian state archive hopes the  information will draw attention to the  soldiers who served in the war  bulgarian  unfortunately in bulgaria  many family histories were interrupted  for decades  some families only know their own  history back to the start of the war  i think that's why our national holidays  are only seen as days off work rather  than celebrations of national pride  the melody of whistling marissa paid  tribute to the battles of the balkan war  this is my family memory from the first  balkan war  which was passed from generation to  generation  the goal of our project to digitalize  and upload the names of more than 48 000  people who died in that war is to help  bulgarian families restore their family  history and re-experience these  historical events as a part of their  family's story  and with that we've come to the end of  this edition of world stories from all  of us here and our partner stations  around the world thanks for watching  don't forget to watch this show again  head to our website at d w dot d e world  stories we'll leave you with this week's  picks of the world see you next time  bye  you
 

